    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Offer Assessment: Is It a Good Offer?\n\nOn paper, **$1.08M in RSUs** looks like a huge headline number, but when broken down, the offer is essentially **a lateral move with near-identical annualized compensation**:\n\n* **Base Salary ($235k vs. $240k):** This is a minor pay cut. The posted range goes up to $271,000, so Google is offering you near the mid-point.\n* **Target Bonus ($47k vs. $36k):** Google targets 20% for L6, compared to Meta's 15% target for IC6.\n* **Annualized Equity ($270k/yr vs. $250k/yr):** $1.08M over 4 years averages out to $270,000/year.\n* **Total Annualized Compensation (Run-Rate):**\n  * **Google:** $235,000 + $47,000 + $270,000 = **~$552,000/year**\n  * **Meta:** $240,000 + $36,000 + $250,000 = **~$526,000/year**\n\nA **~5% annualized increase** does not justify leaving a role where you are happy, established, and performing well.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Nuance: Google\u2019s Front-Loaded Vesting Schedule\n\nGoogle typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for new hires (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** across 4 years, rather than a flat 25% per year):\n\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You would vest ~**$356k/year** in stock. Your Year 1 and Year 2 total comp would be **~$638k/year**.\n* **Years 3 & 4:** Stock vests drop sharply to ~**$237k** (Year 3) and ~**$130k** (Year 4). \n* **The Refresher Trap:** While annual refreshers stack over time, Google\u2019s refresher grants vest evenly (25%/year). Unless you receive top-tier performance ratings, refreshers often struggle to prevent total comp from dipping in Years 3 and 4.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommendation: Negotiate (Do Not Accept As-Is)\n\nBecause Google sought you out and you are content at Meta, **you hold all the leverage**. \n\nYou should **negotiate aggressively**. If Google meets your targets, you get a substantial step up in compensation; if they refuse, you stay comfortably at Meta with no downside.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\n| Component | Initial Offer | Counter Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Eliminates the base pay cut ($240k current) and moves closer to the top of Google's posted band ($271k). |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | Increases annual equity run-rate to ~$312k\u2013$337k/yr, pushing Years 1\u20132 compensation to ~$700k+. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets unvested equity transitions, forfeiture of near-term Meta vesting cycles, and initial risk. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nFrame your negotiation around two points: **you are not actively looking**, and **the current offer does not provide enough delta over your Meta trajectory to warrant leaving**.\n\n#### Sample Response to Recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and love the scope of the role. However, when I look at the compensation breakdown, the annualized package is essentially a lateral move relative to my current trajectory at Meta (where I am currently at $240k base and ~$526k total run-rate).*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking to move and am in a strong position on my team, leaving Meta requires a compensation package that reflects that risk and step forward. To make this an easy decision and sign, I am looking for:*\n>\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** **$255,000** (to reflect a step up from my current $240k base and align with the upper end of the posted band).*\n> 2. * **Equity:** **$1,280,000** across 4 years (to ensure long-term stability past the initial front-loaded vesting window).*\n> 3. * **Sign-on Bonus:** **$75,000** (to account for unvested equity transitions and bridging the move).*\n>\n> *If we can bridge the gap to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and join the team.\"*",
