     "content": "### 1. The Updated Financial Reality\n\nThis is a **major win**. By securing a **$200,000 sign-on bonus**, you didn\u2019t just replace your $200,000 unvested Meta equity\u2014you effectively added $200,000 of pure cash on top of Google\u2019s already front-loaded equity schedule.\n\nHere is how your total compensation projects year-by-year (assuming Google\u2019s standard **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** front-loaded equity schedule):\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | Initial RSUs Vesting | Sign-on Bonus | Estimated Refreshes | **Estimated Total Comp** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $240,000 | $48,000 | ~$356,400 (33%) | $200,000 | $0 | **~$844,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $240,000 | $48,000 | ~$356,400 (33%) | $0 | ~$25,000 | **~$669,400** |\n| **Year 3** | $240,000 | $48,000 | ~$237,600 (22%) | $0 | ~$60,000 | **~$585,600** |\n| **Year 4** | $240,000 | $48,000 | ~$129,600 (12%) | $0 | ~$90,000 | **~$507,600** |\n\n* **4-Year Guaranteed / Baseline Value:** **~$2.43M** (~$608,000/yr average, excluding stock appreciation and refreshes).\n* **Comparison to Meta:** At Meta\u2019s current run-rate ($526k/yr), you would make ~$2.10M over 4 years. \n* **The Delta:** You will pull in **over $300,000 in additional compensation in Year 1 alone**, and roughly **$330,000+ more across 4 years**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should You Take It?\n\n**Financially: Yes, take it.** \n\nA $200,000 cash sign-on for an L6 Product Manager at Google is near the ceiling of what their compensation committee approves without a competing L7 offer. They met your unvested equity concern with liquid cash without reducing your RSU grant.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Non-Financial Trade-offs to Consider\n\nBefore signing, weigh the cultural and career velocity differences:\n\n1. **Product Execution & Culture:**\n   * **Meta (IC6):** Highly execution-focused, top-down clarity, metrics-driven, faster ship cycles. High agency, but can be higher pressure.\n   * **Google (L6):** Highly consensus-driven, matrixed stakeholder management, and generally slower product launch cycles. Lower day-to-day burn, but navigating internal politics can take more energy.\n2. **Promotion Velocity (L6 \u2192 L7 vs. IC6 \u2192 IC7):**\n   * Google L6 to L7 is notoriously difficult and slow compared to Meta, where aggressive shipping can accelerate promotions.\n3. **The Year 3\u20134 Equity Trajectory:**\n   * Because of Google's front-loaded equity, your compensation will peak in Years 1 and 2 ($844k and $669k). By Year 3 and 4, you will feel a natural plateau unless you receive top-tier refresher grants, stock appreciation, or a promotion to L7.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Checklist Before Signing\n\n* **Sign-on Clawback Terms:** Clarify if the $200k is paid in your first paycheck and whether the clawback is prorated over 12 months or 24 months. (A 1-year clawback is standard).\n* **Start Date vs. Meta Vesting:** Check your Meta vesting calendar. If you have an upcoming quarterly vest in the next few weeks, set your Google start date immediately after that vest clears.",
